{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UbBsbbgR9TsZcc1DP48F8V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of gnumfmt behavior:\n\n1. `--invalid=fail` (default): Print error to stderr, print invalid value to stdout, exit 2\n2. `--invalid=warn`: Print warning to stderr, print invalid value to stdout, exit 0\n3. `--invalid=ignore`: No stderr message, print invalid value to stdout, exit 0\n\nCurrent implementation: On error, it prints to stderr but does NOT print the invalid value to stdout. This needs to change - on error, the value should always be output unchanged to stdout.\n\nWait, let me look at how the current code handles errors more carefully. \n\nThe issue is that when `processLine` and `processToken` encounter errors, they return without printing anything to stdout. The main loop catches these errors and prints to stderr, but the original invalid value never gets output. I need to restructure this so that whenever a conversion fails, the original value is always printed to stdout, and then the `--invalid` flag controls whether we also print to stderr and what exit code we use. I need to reconsider how the error handling works in these functions. Currently, processToken and processLine return early on error without printing anything to stdout, but gnumfmt actually prints the original invalid value to stdout regardless of the error. The question is whether to modify these functions to always print (even on failure) or handle the printing in the main loop where the --invalid mode logic already lives. I think modifying the functions themselves is cleaner since it keeps the printing logic centralized, and this actually aligns with gnumfmt's behavior of always outputting something to stdout even when conversion fails.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":920,"cache_read_input_tok