o perfectly match gnumfmt's behavior for every edge case. The existing abort mode tests already pass, so I just need to implement the `--invalid` flag to handle fail, warn, and ignore modes for the main input cases. I'll add the `invalid` field to the options struct and parse the flag with \"abort\" as the default.\n\nWriting error handling logic... I see the issue now—the existing abort mode tests expect no output on error, so I can't just always print. I need to differentiate: abort mode keeps the current behavior of not printing when there's an error, while other modes print the original value and continue processing. Let me refine the approach to handle this distinction properly. For non-abort modes, I need to process all fields even when some fail, keeping the original line for output. I'm creating a separate tolerant version of the field processing function that continues through errors instead of bailing on the first one, always prints output, and returns the initial error encountered.\n\nModifying field processing functions... Writing error handling logic...\n\nActually, I'm realizing this approach is getting messy with all the conditional checks scattered throughout main(). It would be cleaner to have the process functions themselves handle printing and error reporting based on the invalid mode, so main() doesn't need to know about these details. I'm realizing I should create a helper function that handles conversion errors consistently across different contexts. This function would take the original input, the error, and whether we're dealing with a field or line-level error, then apply the right behavior based on the invalid mode—printing the original line when appropriate, logging the error to stderr, and setting the error flag when needed.\n\nWriting error handler... Completing the error handling logic for stdin processing...\n\nNow I need to adjust the field processing functions to respect the invalid mode setting—in abort mode they should fail immediately, but otherwise they'll preserve the original field when an error occurs, print the result anyway, and track the first error encountered.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":240,"cache_read_input_tokens":44347,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":240},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1906c47b-d50a-4d3d-b5b7-1f0ca9abf10d","uuid":"f43946dc-8435-4149-a36f-250777369a00"}
